- 4C plating on thick electrodes: needs electrolyte formulation (σ/t⁺/D overrides), N/P (neg thickness up), negative particle size down? Wait — for plating: the negative electrode surface potential dips below 0 due to: (1) high local current at 4C, (2) concentration polarization in electrolyte (depletion at anode), (3) poor anode kinetics. Levers:
  - Electrolyte conductivity σ up: reduces ohmic drop in electrolyte → higher anode potential during charge... The plating criterion: negative electrode potential φ_s − φ_e at separator interface... φ_s is negative electrode potential; more conductive electrolyte = φ_e drop smaller → η_neg less negative. Yes.
